      "explanation": "The exponent-sum rule lets us write every term using the common factor \\(2^n\\): \\(2^{n+4}=16\\cdot2^n\\) and \\(2^{n+3}=8\\cdot2^n\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\frac{2^{n+4}-2(2^n)}{2(2^{n+3})}\n=\\frac{(16-2)2^n}{16\\cdot2^n}\n=\\frac{14}{16}\n=\\frac78.\n\\]\nThe cancellation is valid because \\(2^n>0\\) for every real \\(n\\). The answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac78}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
      "id": "extract-common-power",
      "sourceRef": "amc/1951_AMC_12_Q24#extract-common-power",
      "title": "Factor out the common power"
    }
